.TH LIBESDM_AUX_CLIENT 3 "2026-08-05" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_aux_client \- wait until the ESDM server asks for entropy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm_aux_client.h>
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_aux_client
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libesdm_aux_client
lets an entropy provider find out when
.BR esdm\-server (8)
wants entropy, instead of pushing data at it on a timer.
.PP
The daemon posts a named POSIX semaphore whenever its auxiliary pool falls
below the level it wants, and publishes the same in its status shared memory
segment. A provider waits for that and inserts
entropy \- through
.BR esdm_rpcc_write_data ()
of
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
or
.BR esdm_egd_client_write_entropy ()
of
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
\- whenever the wait returns. This is meant to be used in a loop, so that the
provider delivers exactly when the daemon has a use for the data.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP
.BI "int esdm_aux_init_wait_for_need_entropy(void);"
Attach to the status segment and the semaphore. Both belong to the daemon, and
this creates neither: while the daemon is not running yet, the wait below
attaches to them once it has created them, and again after it replaced them,
e.g. when it was restarted. A segment is only taken for the daemon's when root,
the owner of the daemon's unprivileged RPC socket or the caller's own user
created it: another user may create one under the well\-known key while the
daemon is not running. Returns 0 on success and a negative errno value on
error.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_aux_fini_wait_for_need_entropy(void);"
Detach from the status segment and the semaphore.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_aux_timedwait_for_need_entropy(struct timespec *" ts ");"
Wait until the daemon asks for entropy, or until the absolute deadline
.I ts
expires, which is measured against
.BR CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
not against
.BR CLOCK_REALTIME .
The return value and
.I errno
convention are those of
.BR sem_clockwait (3).
In particular a return of \-1 with
.I errno
set to
.B ETIMEDOUT
means the deadline passed without the daemon asking, which is not an error but
the caller's cue to loop. Without a running daemon, the wait lasts until the
deadline as well.
.IP
The semaphore is created for the user the daemon runs as, and a provider
running as another user cannot open it. Such a provider watches the status
segment instead, which is noticed within some 50 milliseconds.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <esdm/esdm_aux_client.h>
#include <errno.h>
#include <time.h>

if (esdm_aux_init_wait_for_need_entropy())
	return \-1;

for (;;) {
	struct timespec ts;

	clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
	ts.tv_sec += 60;

	if (esdm_aux_timedwait_for_need_entropy(&ts) == 0) {
		/* the ESDM wants entropy - insert some */
	} else if (errno != ETIMEDOUT) {
		break;
	}
}

esdm_aux_fini_wait_for_need_entropy();
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR sem_clockwait (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
